WEBVTT

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.380
So that's why I said: "It's a bad idea."

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:13.380
You said that?

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.080
I said it, no question! And yet...

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:17.330
Hey...

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:23.670
Yikes, what's with that guy?

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.040
Report him, report him.

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.130
This is your target.

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:09.040
Umataro! Hey, Umataro!

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:14.630
Good morning, Ochanomizu-san.
Please enter the security code.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:15.710
Yeah, yeah...

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.040
Ten, three, one.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.210
Currently, entry to this laboratory is forbidden.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:28.080
Six, it's me. Me!

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:31.630
Requesting your name and affiliation, please.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:36.000
That damn Umataro. He must've fiddled
with this guy's memory again.

00:02:36.670 --> 00:02:39.000
My name is Hiroshi Ochanomizu.

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:43.630
I'm a research student in Lab 7 of
Nerima University's robotics master's program!

00:02:43.790 --> 00:02:47.630
Incidentally, I'm one of the guys who made you,
which makes me your creator. And don't forget it!

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:49.500
Understood.

00:02:49.630 --> 00:02:52.920
Verification in the database was successful.

00:02:54.130 --> 00:02:55.380
Alright, I'm coming in now.

00:02:55.380 --> 00:02:56.830
Hey, what gives?!

00:02:57.040 --> 00:03:01.500
Umataro Tenma-san is currently
in the middle of REM sleep.

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:05.460
Please wait 15 minutes until he wakes up.

00:03:06.290 --> 00:03:08.420
Wake up, Umataro!

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.290
We're gonna be late for the regular briefing session!

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.080
Alright, he's up!

00:03:18.290 --> 00:03:25.880
When it comes to the robotics field, Nerima University
is on the cutting edge of research in Japan,

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:30.790
and I hear they also pursue academic-industrial
alliances with a variety of companies.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.670
Okay, go ahead.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:42.170
However, above all else I should really focus
on the fact that this place is a treasure trove.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:46.170
The regular briefing session will take place
in Building A's Main Assembly Hall.

00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:47.580
Thank you.

00:04:30.170 --> 00:04:34.630
Of course, a certain thrill inevitably
comes along with securing the treasure.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:40.500
Ochanomizu-kun!

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:42.880
I brought some snacks!

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:46.630
Presently, Hiroshi Ochanomizu-san is not here.

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:48.880
Huh? He's not?

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:54.000
Usually I can get an unpopular guy like him
to fall for me in no time,

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.630
but I'm having trouble
for some odd reason this time...

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:00.210
That buddy of his, Tenma-kun,
is proving to be an unexpected obstacle.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:07.750
Hey!

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:12.250
I'm not sure where you're from, Kid...
But elementary-school students aren't allowed in here!

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:15.040
I'm not in elementary school.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:16.540
Eh?

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:18.080
I'm Ran...

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.790
Ran Ochanomizu, a second-year high-school student.

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:24.210
Eh? Ochanomizu...?

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:29.080
Eh?! You're Ochanomizu-kun's younger sister?
And you're in HIGH SCHOOL?!

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:33.210
Sorry, I'm sorry.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:35.540
Ran-chan, so you're in your
high school's robotics club, huh?

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:45.460
And this little guy here is F14?
Why did you name him that?

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:48.290
Because we found him on February 14.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:52.330
Ah, the "F" comes from "February."
Was that your idea, Ran-chan?

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:54.460
Umataro's.

00:05:54.460 --> 00:05:58.750
Tenma-kun's? Ah, yeah, I can picture that.

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:01.580
So, did you find any good parts?

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:12.580
A compact actuator. Buying one of these
would cost 4,500 yen.

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:14.710
A roller gear reducer.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:17.080
It's an older type, but it gets four stars on the forums.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.250
40-millimeter cooling fans.

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:23.250
A restricted-transmission-type infrared sensor.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:28.330
An aluminum battery unit and boosters.
Micro switches and a switch box.

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:30.750
A servo driver, a six-speed gearbox.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:35.580
Metal L bearings. Ultra-low-head bolts
with hexalobular holes.

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:36.630
Incredible...

00:06:43.830 --> 00:06:48.630
Wow... Isn't this a practically-new servo motor?!

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:53.460
To think you can find stuff like this in that scrapheap...

00:06:53.920 --> 00:06:57.000
Oh, I see! This place is
a real treasure trove for you, eh?

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.080
Ran-chan, you're amazing!

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:06.630
I have a soft spot for genius kids, you know...

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:09.130
Oh! Speaking of amazing things...

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:12.710
He's called Six-kun, right?
The robot you've got here, I mean.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:16.380
That was quite the feat he pulled off at Mecha City
the other day. You know about it?

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:17.920
Yeah.

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:20.210
Even though he caused quite the commotion,

00:07:20.330 --> 00:07:26.790
I heard that those two weren't only not blamed for it,
but that they were even given a bonus.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:28.460
Is that so?

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:32.460
When I told my older brother,
he was even more surprised than I thought he'd be.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.290
Speaking of which, where are those two?

00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:36.790
Don't know. They weren't here when I arrived.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:40.460
Ah, that's right!

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:44.080
Today must be the graduate school's
regular briefing session!

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:46.790
I'm gonna go take a look, okay?!

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:51.670
Oh, yes. I'm Motoko Tsutsumi, a second-year student
in the robotics department.

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:55.460
Ochanomizu-kun is, uh...

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:59.250
...still just a friend for now, I guess.

00:08:05.630 --> 00:08:06.420
Yo.

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:08.500
Hi.

00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:11.130
Yo. Hi. Yo. Hi.

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:14.960
Yo. Hi. Yo. Hi. Yo. Yo. Yo. Yo. Yo. Yo.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:21.460
Ran-chan, F14-kun, see you later!

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:43.460
Now that's Lab 1 for you, huh?

00:08:43.460 --> 00:08:47.290
Their report is what our guests from robotics-related
corporations, both foreign and domestic,

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:49.460
came here to see, after all.

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:51.210
Yes, indeed.

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:54.130
However, it seems Tsutsumi-kun isn't in attendance.

00:08:54.580 --> 00:08:58.130
In that case, he must be devoting all his energy
to joint academic-industrial research.

00:08:58.330 --> 00:09:02.710
At any rate, he's a genius who's
the pride of Nerima University. Right...?

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:09.630
P-pardon me, Professor Saruta.

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:12.630
That guy's an oddball, you know?

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:15.290
But he holds vast authority...

00:09:17.330 --> 00:09:18.920
I'd expect as much from Lab 1.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:22.920
Within this university, no, within Japan
they're among the super-elite.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:24.130
Hmph.

00:09:24.750 --> 00:09:28.040
That's exactly the kind of stuff
guys with money like them would think up.

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:34.880
Still, why the heck doesn't Lab 1
have their leader, Moriya Tsutsumi, here?

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:36.460
Eh? Oh, you're right.

00:09:36.580 --> 00:09:38.460
I don't see Moriya-san anywhere.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:45.500
But, the truth of it is that,
even if Moriya-san doesn't attend the briefing session,

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:49.290
it's not like Lab 1's budget would actually get reduced.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.290
Hmph! I don't like it one bit!

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:53.290
Ah, that's right.

00:09:53.540 --> 00:09:57.500
Now that I think about it,
I wonder what kind of research those two are doing.

00:10:02.040 --> 00:10:04.750
Lab 7... Lab 7 is...

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:12.330
Bewusst...

00:10:17.330 --> 00:10:21.080
So the rotational energy
captured from the actuator is, uh...

00:10:21.080 --> 00:10:23.380
by controlling it in 256 distinct steps,

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:27.460
the fluctuation and compensation of the vertical
movement in the aforementioned reference No. 1 is...

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:31.380
Uh... What comes next after that again?

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:36.960
Ah! That caused a reduction of three
in the value of the horizontal kinetic energy.

00:10:37.330 --> 00:10:38.880
Um, the numerical formula is exactly as written out
on the materials in front of you...

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:42.250
Pfft! Their budget will be reduced, no doubt.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:43.130
Ah, it's not?! Oh, please excuse me!

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:45.500
We don't exactly know how it's going to turn out, do we?

00:10:46.170 --> 00:10:48.380
We'll be fine...
Uh, so the actual value is negative 3,000...

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:50.170
Uh, so the actual value is negative 3,000...

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:52.380
We're geniuses, after all.

00:10:57.330 --> 00:11:00.580
Tch, they changed it, huh? Stiff security...

00:11:36.170 --> 00:11:38.540
Eh, s-so that means...

00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:43.630
With our team's thought-fractal
processing AI here at Lab 6,

00:11:45.330 --> 00:11:47.040
Pfft! What a load of crap!

00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:48.290
uh, by processing the seemingly infinite number
of events that could occur using fractal geometry,

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:51.000
Ugh, it's almost our turn now...

00:11:51.000 --> 00:11:51.750
Ugh, it's almost our turn now...
we succeeded in dispersing it
into third-gen thinking attractors,

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:54.670
Ah, I'm so nervous!

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:57.170
and defined what it means to be able to
cope with every conceivable situation.

00:11:57.170 --> 00:12:00.080
Get it together, Hiroshi. It'll be alright.

00:12:00.080 --> 00:12:00.420
And that now concludes our presentation here at Lab 6.
Thank you for your attention.

00:12:00.420 --> 00:12:03.920
Say what you want, but we're geniuses.

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:06.420
Ah, that's right!

00:12:09.790 --> 00:12:11.960
Ah, this really calms me down.

00:12:12.130 --> 00:12:14.460
Next up, Lab 7!

00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:17.750
Alright! Let's show 'em what we've got, Hiroshi!

00:12:17.750 --> 00:12:18.920
O-okay...

00:12:20.080 --> 00:12:21.170
Lab 7 is next?

00:12:21.170 --> 00:12:22.540
Those troublemakers, right?

00:12:22.540 --> 00:12:23.580
I'm off to the toilet.

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:24.670
What's that about?

00:12:25.080 --> 00:12:27.750
Hmph! Just you wait!

00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:45.790
No trespassing.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:47.710
And no shoes inside either.

00:13:02.830 --> 00:13:06.080
Tactical program: shift to two-zero-one-five.

00:13:24.500 --> 00:13:25.830
In short...

00:13:26.130 --> 00:13:32.420
All the research teams besides us here at Lab 7
talk about researching autonomous AI,

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:33.630
but if you look under the hood,

00:13:33.630 --> 00:13:35.500
by hypothesizing possible events

00:13:35.500 --> 00:13:39.630
and then simply increasing the number of programs
to deal with said events...

00:13:40.040 --> 00:13:44.000
...it follows that they are devoted to presenting
the APPEARANCE of autonomy!

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:46.540
They are only presenting the mere appearance of it!

00:13:46.540 --> 00:13:50.250
If you ask me, that's what I call a sham!
You bunch of fakes!

00:13:50.250 --> 00:13:51.880
Phonies! Frauds!

00:13:52.040 --> 00:13:53.630
What kind of bogus autonomy is that?!

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:56.250
Umataro, you're taking it too far...

00:13:56.790 --> 00:13:57.880
However...!

00:13:57.880 --> 00:14:03.500
The "Bewusstsein System," which is our research theme
here at Lab 7, is different!

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:04.710
Fundamentally so!

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:07.750
Bewusstsein! In other words, "Ego-Consciousness"!

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:15.000
Giving a machine with no soul the notion of
Ego-consciousness is the only path to true autonomy!

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:16.630
Soul...?

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:28.880
What lies beyond Ego-consciousness is in fact the Heart.

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:32.750
Speaking of the Heart...
Kindness, compassion, sorrow...

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.380
It's the place where the various emotions
are gathered together.

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:37.210
Speaking of those emotions,

00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:42.960
by building up a vast set of experiences
with things like crying, laughing, and getting angry,

00:14:43.130 --> 00:14:48.790
these become the barometer by which
one can ascertain the optimal way to respond to others.

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:36.210
In addition, making a clear distinction
between Oneself and Others is vital.

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:41.420
In other words, Ego-consciousness
is the first step toward the concept of the Heart.

00:15:42.960 --> 00:15:47.080
Upon the formation of the Heart,
which binds together Oneself with Others,

00:15:47.210 --> 00:15:51.000
it is in fact Priority that becomes
the criterion of judgment!

00:15:52.920 --> 00:15:58.920
Namely, it is the attitude whereby one recognizes
what is important to oneself and decides to protect it!

00:15:59.210 --> 00:16:03.040
Sometimes, that even means putting oneself in danger!

00:16:03.420 --> 00:16:06.540
That could very well also be referred to
as Love or Emotion.

00:16:07.290 --> 00:16:10.580
It is indeed affection toward others
that is the foundation of the Heart,

00:16:10.580 --> 00:16:12.790
and Ego-consciousness is the starting point for that!

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:18.790
Science, which has forgotten about the Heart,
lacks the kind of love that pursues happiness!

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:23.130
What is he saying?

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:24.830
I've got no idea...

00:16:25.080 --> 00:16:29.080
This here is the department of Robotics,
not Philosophy or Psychology!

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:32.330
Is everything alright with those two...?

00:17:09.500 --> 00:17:10.670
Oh...?

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:30.920
Six...

00:18:31.540 --> 00:18:35.710
High-Speed Beat Punch! And... Impact Attack!

00:18:36.000 --> 00:18:39.920
Both of those were equipped as features
that can be touted as the very strongest!

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:41.880
What... is that?

00:18:42.000 --> 00:18:48.420
The piston mechanism of the arms of A106, the robot
we developed, makes these special techniques possible!

00:18:48.420 --> 00:18:49.710
Hey, Umataro...

00:18:49.710 --> 00:18:50.380
What is it?

00:18:50.540 --> 00:18:52.960
You don't need to go into
the workings of Six's arms right now.

00:18:52.960 --> 00:18:55.880
Wrong! It's related to what I'll be talking about next!

00:18:56.380 --> 00:18:57.880
Ahem!

00:19:28.420 --> 00:19:29.710
A robot!

00:19:33.500 --> 00:19:35.500
Stay put, please.

00:19:35.750 --> 00:19:38.540
Opponent's target is Ran-san.

00:19:38.670 --> 00:19:40.500
Probability: high.

00:19:40.630 --> 00:19:42.630
Running away: difficult.

00:19:42.750 --> 00:19:43.830
It will pursue.

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:48.880
It is not a human.

00:19:49.210 --> 00:19:50.250
A formidable enemy.

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:54.330
Ran-san, protect you, I will.

00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:59.420
So, stay right there, please.

00:20:00.130 --> 00:20:03.790
Stay directly behind me, please.

00:20:39.040 --> 00:20:40.920
Unable to detect it.

00:20:41.040 --> 00:20:42.000
It left.

00:20:43.960 --> 00:20:46.670
Are you injured, Ran-san?

00:20:52.130 --> 00:20:54.080
Jeez! Are you kidding me?!

00:20:56.380 --> 00:20:59.170
I was still in the middle of presenting...

00:20:59.170 --> 00:21:02.580
Those damn professors!
They cut me off before I was finished!

00:21:04.040 --> 00:21:06.130
We're finished.

00:21:06.130 --> 00:21:10.250
Our research funding will be discontinued
and we'll be kicked out of our lab,

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:14.080
and Lab 7 itself will be demolished.

00:21:15.040 --> 00:21:16.880
Nope, that's not gonna happen!

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:22.420
Umataro... how is that you're
always able to stay so confident?

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:26.710
Hiroshi, it is you who has too little confidence!

00:21:37.670 --> 00:21:39.920
Oh, Ran, you're here?

00:21:40.420 --> 00:21:41.080
Big Brother!

00:21:41.080 --> 00:21:44.290
Head home now. Dad will get worried
about you again, you know.

00:21:45.500 --> 00:21:49.380
Huh?! Aren't things even more
scattered about than they were before?

00:21:49.380 --> 00:21:51.290
What's that all about, Six?

00:21:52.920 --> 00:21:54.710
Um, Brother...

00:21:54.830 --> 00:21:58.080
Seems this guy is still a bit out of wack.

00:21:59.210 --> 00:22:00.790
Alright, come along!

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:05.500
For now, let's try resetting his memory.

00:22:05.500 --> 00:22:07.380
Yeah, good idea.

00:22:07.380 --> 00:22:09.790
And... Ah! The door!

00:22:09.790 --> 00:22:11.630
It's totally gone!

00:22:11.630 --> 00:22:12.170
Hmph...

00:22:13.250 --> 00:22:16.920
Just what exactly was that robot?

00:22:17.080 --> 00:22:20.670
As if trying to test out something, it fought with Six.

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:22.830
And then it just left.

00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:29.130
Was its target Six? Or was it me...?

00:22:32.210 --> 00:22:35.540
But... there is one thing I can say:

00:22:36.330 --> 00:22:39.630
It was then that Six became a hero.

00:22:40.380 --> 00:22:43.080
He became my hero.

00:22:58.460 --> 00:22:59.880
Good night.

00:24:31.580 --> 00:24:33.250
Ah, what should I do?

00:24:33.250 --> 00:24:36.630
What's the matter, Ochanomizu-kun?
Could it be that you're lovesick or something?

00:24:36.630 --> 00:24:37.710
Motoko-san!

00:24:37.710 --> 00:24:40.580
No, it's just that Umataro bought parts
with no regard for our budget,

00:24:40.580 --> 00:24:42.330
so our research funds are all gone.

00:24:42.330 --> 00:24:44.750
Ah, so you're perpetually strapped for cash, huh?

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:46.710
In that case, I know a great part-time job for you!

00:24:46.710 --> 00:24:49.000
Part-time job?! Go on and tell me! What kind of job?!

00:24:49.000 --> 00:24:50.170
Well, you see...

00:24:50.170 --> 00:24:52.960
Next time on ATOM THE BEGINNING:
"Pursuing the Respective Leads"

00:24:52.960 --> 00:24:54.750
Wanna do a bit of sleuthing?
